Code and data for "Do Language Models Know Which Constraints Belong Together?": zero-marginal groups in Dantzig-Wolfe reformulation, the JointDW search, and the experiments on MIPLIB-NL, rich models and benchmark instances. - junbolian/JointDW
Dantzig-Wolfe reformulation strengthens the linear relaxation of a mixed-integer program by convexifying a chosen set of constraint families, and every candidate costs a column generation run. The choice is hard when families strengthen the bound only together, because a search that adds one family at a time then stops at the linear relaxation.
@misc{lian2026jointdw, title = {Do Language Models Know Which Constraints Belong Together?}, author = {Lian, Junbo Jacob and Chen, Kaixuan and Qin, Hanzhang and Chen, Huiling and Zhang, Chaoyu and Teo, Chung-Piaw}, year = {2026}, note = {Preprint}, eprint = {2611.05726}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {cs.LG}, url = {https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05726} }
When each evaluation may convexify at most s of k families, every method that observes only bounds needs a covering number of evaluations, of order kw, to find a hidden group of w families. A prediction that misses e members reduces the cost to Θ(ke) through one staged search that does not need to know e, and the search costs only a lower-order term more when the prediction is entirely wrong.

